"Centroid and second moment of area from numpy arrays
'The parts of a built-up section are numpy arrays in a <b>#python</b> block: areas, centroid heights and the parallel axis theorem. The block exports the centroid <i>y_c</i> (mm) and <i>I_x</i> (mm^4); Calcpad then works out a bending stress.
#python
import numpy as np

b = np.array([200.0, 12.0, 150.0])      # widths, mm
h = np.array([16.0, 300.0, 20.0])       # heights, mm
y = np.array([8.0, 166.0, 324.0])       # part centroids above the base, mm

A = b * h
y_c = float((A * y).sum() / A.sum())
I_own = b * h**3 / 12
I_x = float((I_own + A * (y - y_c)**2).sum())

print("Areas (mm^2):", A.tolist())
print("Centroid:", round(y_c, 2), "mm")
print("Second moment of area:", round(I_x / 1e6, 2), "x 10^6 mm^4")
export(y_c=y_c, I_x=I_x, A_tot=float(A.sum()))
#end python
"Bending stress
M = 40kN*m
y_t = 340mm - y_c*1mm
I = I_x*1mm^4
σ = M*y_t/I|MPa
